{"id":"a9a9a5eb-0163-4837-b57d-ea116877e004","object":"chat.completion","created":1784999794,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    core_distance = case[\\\"core_distance\\\"]\\n    distances = case[\\\"distances\\\"]\\n    edges = []\\n    for entry in distances:\\n        u, v, d = entry\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        w = max(core_distance[u], core_distance[v], d)\\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort()\\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    needed = len(nodes) - 1\\n    for w, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == needed:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != needed:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")\\n    return {\\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that computes a Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) of a mutual reachability graph. The case payload contains: nodes (list of node IDs), core_distance (dict mapping node->core distance), distances (list of [node1, node2, distance]). For each pair, compute mutual reachability = max(core1, core2, distance). Then construct graph edges with weight = mutual reachability. Need to compute MST using Kruskal's algorithm with deterministic tie-breaking: sort edges by weight, then by node IDs lexicographically. Use union-find. Return JSON with \"mst_edges\": list of [node1, node2, weight] (each as a 3-element list: two strings, one number), and \"total_weight\": sum of weights. Ensure edges are canonical (undirected): lower node ID first? The input nodes are strings like \"p0\", \"p1\", etc. For tie-breaking, we need to sort by endpoints: typically sort pair such that first node < second node lexicographically. Then sort edges by (weight, first_node, second_node). Also ensure we handle all pairs? The distances list may not include all pairs? It should include all unordered pairs? The examples show 10 distances for 5 nodes (complete graph). But spec says \"registered distance matrices\" but we only have distances list. Possibly the distances list contains all pairs? We should assume it contains all distinct pairs (i.e., for each unordered pair, exactly one entry). If not, we'd need to handle missing edges? But the problem says \"reject disconnected inputs\" - we can compute MST only if graph is connected. So we must ensure the graph is complete? Actually distances list might be incomplete. We need to compute mutual reachability for each pair in the distances list. If any pair is missing, then the graph might be disconnected. But the spec says \"compute mutual-reachability edge weights\" for each pair. The input format may not guarantee completeness; we can compute MST only on given edges. If the graph is disconnected, raise ValueError. That's fine.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate payload: check nodes, core_distance, distances are present. Create dict for core distances.\n2. Build list of edges: for each entry [u, v, d] in distances, compute mutual_reachability = max(core_distance[u], core_distance[v], d). Add edge as (u, v, weight). Normalize: ensure u < v string compare (since node IDs are strings). Also keep original order? For tie-breaking we need canonical representation. We'll store sorted pairs.\n3. Sort edges by (weight, u, v).\n4. Union-Find: initialize parent dict for each node. Also rank (or size) for path compression.\n5. mst_edges = [], total_weight = 0. Iterate through sorted edges. For each (u, v, w), find roots. If different, union and add edge (u, v, w) to mst_edges, add w to total_weight. Stop when len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1.\n6. After processing all edges, if len(mst_edges) < len(nodes)-1, raise ValueError(\"Graph is disconnected\").\n7. Return dict: {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}.\n\nEdge case: there may be multiple edges between same nodes? The distances list should have unique pairs. But we can ignore duplicates if any. In case of duplicate, the sort will pick the smallest weight first; subsequent same pair will be skipped because union-find will find same root. That's fine.\n\nMake sure to not mutate input.\n\nWrite solve(case) accordingly.\n\nNow output as JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON string. Need to escape quotes, backslashes, newlines.\n\nLet me write the Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    core_distance = case[\"core_distance\"]\n    distances = case[\"distances\"]\n    \n    # Build edges with mutual reachability\n    edges = []\n    for entry in distances:\n        u, v, d = entry\n        # canonical order for tie-breaking later\n        if u > v:\n            u, v = v, u\n        w = max(core_distance[u], core_distance[v], d)\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\n    \n    # Sort: weight, then u, then v\n    edges.sort()\n    \n    # Union-Find\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\n    \n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n        return True\n    \n    mst_edges = []\n    total_weight = 0.0\n    needed = len(nodes) - 1\n    \n    for w, u, v in edges:\n        if union(u, v):\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\n            total_weight += w\n            if len(mst_edges) == needed:\n                break\n    \n    if len(mst_edges) != needed:\n        raise ValueError(\"Graph is disconnected\")\n    \n    return {\n        \"mst_edges\": mst_edges,\n        \"total_weight\": total_weight\n    }\n```\n\nCheck: The output schema requires mst_edges: list of 3-element lists: [string, string, number]. total_weight: number. That matches.\n\nNow need to produce JSON output with key \"implementation\". The Python source must be a string with escaped characters. I'll write the code as a string. Use triple quotes to avoid escaping? But JSON requires escaping newlines as \\n and quotes as \\\". So I'll write the code using double quotes inside and escape properly. Better to use a raw string? No, need to encode. I'll write the Python code as a multiline string, then create a JSON string by replacing newlines with \\n and double quotes with \\\". Could be error-prone. Since I'm writing the response manually, I need to output the JSON directly. I'll construct the JSON object with the implementation key and the string value containing the Python source with proper escaping.\n\nAlternatively, I can output using a code block? The STRICT SUBMISSION ENVELOPE says no Markdown code fences, entire response must be a JSON object. So I must output raw JSON.\n\nI'll write the Python code with single quotes for strings to minimize escaping needed inside double-quoted JSON string? Actually JSON string delimiters are double quotes. If the Python code uses double quotes, they need to be escaped as \\\". However, the Python code in the solution uses double quotes for strings like \"nodes\" etc. So I need to escape those. Also backslashes are not present. Newlines need to be \\n.\n\nLet me compose the JSON string manually. I'll take the Python code and escape it. The code has newlines; I'll replace each newline with \\n. Also each double quote inside the code becomes \\\". Note that the code itself may contain escaped quotes? No.\n\nI'll write the Python code as a continuous line with \\n. That is messy but necessary.\n\nBetter: I'll use a JSON serializer in my mind. Let me write the Python source as a raw string and then manually escape. But I need to be accurate.\n\nI'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    core_distance = case[\\\"core_distance\\\"]\\n    distances = case[\\\"distances\\\"]\\n    edges = []\\n    for entry in distances:\\n        u, v, d = entry\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        w = max(core_distance[u], core_distance[v], d)\\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort()\\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    needed = len(nodes) - 1\\n    for w, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == needed:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != needed:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")\\n    return {\\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}\\n\"\n}\n\nCheck: All double quotes in the code are escaped as \\\". Newlines represented as \\n. The string must be valid JSON. Also there is a line \"w = max(core_distance[u], core_distance[v], d)\" - that's fine. The union function uses \"return False\" etc. The final return uses dictionary with keys mst_edges and total_weight. The outer JSON object has key \"implementation\".\n\nNow ensure that the entire response is exactly that JSON object. No extra whitespace? The specification says first non-whitespace character must be { and last must be }. So we can have whitespace inside JSON? Yes, but not necessary. I'll output minified to avoid issues. However, the JSON string value contains newline escapes; those are fine.\n\nI'll produce the JSON object without extra whitespace.\n\nBut check: The implementation string contains \\n for newlines. In the actual code, we need the function definition with proper indentation. The string will be interpreted by Python when eval'ed? The specification says \"return Python source defining solve(case)\". So the string will be used as source code. The source code must be syntactically valid Python. The string as I wrote should be valid: each line ends with newline, indentation is spaces. However, note that the line \"def solve(case):\" must be followed by indented lines. The string contains \\n after each line. The indentation is preserved: after \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n\" - that's 4 spaces before nodes. That's fine.\n\nPotential issue: I used single quotes for strings in Python code? No, all strings are double quotes inside the code. That's fine because they are escaped as \\\" inside the JSON string.\n\nNow output the JSON. I'll write it as a single line to avoid any extra whitespace issues.\n\nBut the user might have a parser that expects exactly that. I'll output:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    core_distance = case[\\\"core_distance\\\"]\\n    distances = case[\\\"distances\\\"]\\n    edges = []\\n    for entry in distances:\\n        u, v, d = entry\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        w = max(core_distance[u], core_distance[v], d)\\n        edges.append((w, u, v))\\n    edges.sort()\\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    needed = len(nodes) - 1\\n    for w, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, w])\\n            total_weight += w\\n            if len(mst_edges) == needed:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != needed:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")\\n    return {\\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight}\\n\"}\n\nI should double-check the escaping: Every \" in the code is preceded by backslash. Also the newlines are \\n. The final line ends with \\n before closing quote. That should be fine.\n\nAlso note: The code includes \"raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")\" - the escaped quotes inside that: raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\") - but in the JSON string, the backslash before the quote will be interpreted as escape for the quote character. So the resulting string will have a literal backslash? Actually JSON string: \"raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")\" will be interpreted as: raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\") - that is, the backslashes are part of the string? Wait: In JSON, backslash is an escape character. To include a literal backslash, you need double backslash. But we want the Python code to contain a backslash before each inner double quote? No, we don't want backslash in the Python code. The Python code should be: raise ValueError(\"Graph is disconnected\"). So in the JSON string, we need to escape the double quotes inside the code. That means we write \\\" for each double quote. That will produce a literal double quote in the decoded string. But the backslash is only for JSON escaping; it doesn't appear in the decoded string. So \"raise ValueError(\\\"Graph is disconnected\\\")\" in JSON decodes to: raise ValueError(\"Graph is disconnected\"). That is correct. So no double backslash needed. However, when we have \\\" inside the JSON string, that yields a double quote. So I need to ensure that every double quote in the Python code is preceded by a single backslash in the JSON string. That's what I did.\n\nAlso, for the newline \\n: that is escaped as \\n in JSON string, which decodes to actual newline. Good.\n\nPotential issue: In the line \"def find(x):\" there is a colon, no issue. In \"return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}\" there are double quotes inside. Those need to be escaped. I wrote: \\\"mst_edges\\\": mst_edges, \\\"total_weight\\\": total_weight. Yes.\n\nNow I'll produce the final response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2017,"completion_tokens":3786,"total_tokens":5803,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1920},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3372},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1920,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":97},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}